      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I apologize for the excessive goblin metaphors; I can certainly adjust the tone to be more technical and direct regarding your ceramic analysis.\n\nTo distinguish between oxidized and reduced firing in Middle Bronze Age ceramics, you must look at the specific chemical states of iron oxides within the clay body and the glaze/slip.\n\n**1. Core Fabric Analysis (The Clay Body)**\n*   **Oxidized Firing:** In an oxygen-rich atmosphere, iron exists primarily as **hematite (Fe₂O₃)**. This results in a core color ranging from **buff, tan, to reddish-brown**. The clay feels relatively hard and non-porous.\n*   **Reduced Firing:** In an oxygen-poor atmosphere, carbon monoxide reduces the iron to **magnetite (Fe₃O₄)** or **wüstite (FeO)**. This creates a distinct **dark grey, black, or deep brown** core. You may also see \"spongy\" or porous textures in the core if the reduction was severe, as the iron oxide structure collapses.\n\n**2. Surface Slip and Glaze Analysis**\n*   **Oxidized Surface:** If the slip or gl",
